<speak>
<p>We know that the order is four u squared. Separate the power of two in u by writing u equals two to the s minus one, times w, where s is at least one and w is odd. Substituting this into the order gives n equals two to the two s, times w squared. We will keep the power of two as a cycle length, and collect the odd part into the coefficients.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Reduce every exponent in h of X modulo two to the two s. To picture the grouping, consider the hypothetical order thirty-six case. Here u is three, s is one, and w is three. The shorter cycle has four positions. The positions zero, four, eight, and so on through thirty-two all arrive at residue zero. Each of the other three residues also collects nine positions. These are symbolic signs from a hypothetical row; we have not constructed an order thirty-six Hadamard matrix.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>In general, the coefficient a sub r is the sum of h sub r plus ell times two to the two s, as ell runs from zero to w squared minus one. Call the new polynomial T of X. Each new coefficient is a sum of exactly w squared signs. Modulo two, both signs are one. An odd number of them therefore has odd sum. So every a sub r is an odd integer, although it need not still be plus or minus one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Why does the product identity survive? The shorter cycle length divides n. Reducing exponents is therefore a ring map: it respects addition and multiplication. It also sends the inverse of X to the inverse of its image, so it respects star. Thus h maps to T, and h star maps to T star.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Apply this same map to h times h star equals n. We obtain T times T star equals two to the two s, times w squared, in the integer group ring of the shorter cycle. The number of positions has decreased, but the scalar on the right has stayed exactly the same.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>We now carry two facts forward: two to the two s odd coefficients, and this unchanged scalar norm. If s is at least two, the next steps compare opposite coefficients and repeatedly fold the cycle. Their contradiction will force s to equal one. In the original notation, that will say u equals w and is odd. For now, we have prepared the shorter cycle on which that descent can work.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
